WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.890
In twelve fifty-eight, Baghdad was destroyed and the caliph was trampled to death wrapped in a carpet.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:09.700
Then the Mongols sent a letter to Cairo demanding surrender.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.190
The sultan answered by cutting the envoys in two.

4
00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:22.760
Two years later, beside a spring in Galilee, an army of men who had been bought and sold in the slave markets as boys faced the force that no one had been able to stop.

5
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.010
They say that in February 1258, the waters of the Tigris ran in two colors for days.

6
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.900
One was ink, because the books thrown into the river from Baghdad's libraries had darkened the water.

7
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.430
The other was blood.

8
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:52.120
How much of this story is true, and how much is a lament composed later, we cannot say for certain today.

9
00:00:52.120 --> 00:01:03.930
What we do know is this: the city that had been the seat of the Abbasid caliphs for some 500 years, the city seen as the heart of the Islamic world, surrendered after a siege of a few weeks to the army of Hulagu,

10
00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.620
grandson of Genghis Khan.

11
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:14.110
The last caliph, al-Musta'sim, was by most accounts rolled up in a carpet and trampled to death beneath the hooves of horses.

12
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.720
Mongol belief held that spilling a ruler's blood on the ground brought bad luck.

13
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.050
Even the manner of his death was part of a ritual.

14
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.900
Baghdad was only the beginning.

15
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.550
In January 1260, Aleppo fell.

16
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.680
Its walls were breached, and most of its people were put to the sword.

17
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.490
In March, Damascus opened its gates without resistance.

18
00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:44.300
The Ayyubid ruler of Syria, an-Nasir Yusuf, was a descendant of Saladin, yet he could find no choice but to flee.

19
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.070
Mongol advance units pushed south into Palestine, as far as Gaza.

20
00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:54.800
Anyone looking at the map saw the same thing: Mesopotamia and Syria were gone.

21
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.450
Egypt was the last great Islamic state still standing.

22
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.300
It was then that a letter reached Cairo.

23
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.310
Hulagu's envoys had brought it.

24
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:09.400
The Mamluk historian al-Maqrizi recorded its text some two centuries later.

25
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.690
Its language read less like a letter to a foreign ruler than like a verdict.

26
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.780
Hulagu presented himself as a punishment sent down to earth by Heaven.

27
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:27.710
He reminded the masters of Egypt of what had happened to Baghdad and Aleppo, and wrote that there was nowhere to run, that neither fortresses nor armies would protect them.

28
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.680
There were two choices: surrender, or be destroyed.

29
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.490
The man who read the letter was Sayf al-Din Qutuz.

30
00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:43.140
He had taken the throne only a few months earlier, in November 1259, and the way he had done it could hardly be called legitimate.

31
00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:50.950
He had deposed the previous sultan's son, still a child, arguing that no child could rule the country in the face of the Mongol threat.

32
00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:55.080
Most of the emirs in Cairo submitted to him only grudgingly.

33
00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.130
Qutuz himself was a slave, or rather, a man who had become a soldier while still a slave.

34
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:09.900
By his own account, he was in fact a prince of the Khwarazmian dynasty, the nephew of Jalal al-Din, the last Khwarazmshah to fight the Mongols.

35
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:17.870
When his family was scattered by the Mongol storm, he was taken captive and passed from hand to hand through the slave markets until he reached Egypt.

36
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.800
We have no document to prove this story, but his contemporaries believed it.

37
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.970
If it is true, then Qutuz read this letter as the child of a family whose dynasty the Mongols had destroyed.

38
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:34.260
The word mamluk means "one who is owned" in Arabic.

39
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:41.590
The Ayyubid sultans of Egypt, especially as-Salih Ayyub, had built the core of their armies from young slaves bought at market.

40
00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:46.360
Most of these boys were Kipchak Turks brought from the steppes north of the Black Sea.

41
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:54.010
In Cairo they were housed in barracks, converted to Islam, and trained for years in horsemanship, archery and the lance.

42
00:03:54.010 --> 00:04:01.180
When their training was complete they were freed, but they remained a military brotherhood, bound to their masters and to one another.

43
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:07.550
In 1250, these soldiers overthrew their own masters' dynasty and seized power for themselves.

44
00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:11.360
And here lies the strangest part of the story.

45
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.930
In the 1230s and 1240s, Mongol armies had swept across the Kipchak steppe.

46
00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:23.300
Tens of thousands of young Kipchak captives ended up in the slave markets, and some of them were sold to Egypt.

47
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:31.110
In other words, the flow of people that fed the growth of the Mamluk army in Cairo had largely been created by the Mongol conquests.

48
00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:37.000
Many of the men reading Hulagu's letter were people the Mongols had torn from their homelands as children.

49
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.690
Qutuz's decision was harsh.

50
00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:46.380
He had the envoys executed and their severed heads hung on Bab Zuwayla, the Zuwayla Gate, Cairo's southern gate.

51
00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:56.590
In the language of diplomacy, this meant only one thing: the Mongols held the inviolability of envoys to be sacred, and they never left a city that killed their envoys standing.

52
00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:00.560
Qutuz had deliberately destroyed any chance of compromise.

53
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.850
From then on, surrender was no longer an option for Egypt.

54
00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.060
Only war remained.

55
00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:18.710
But for there to be a war, the enemy first had to reach the gates of Cairo, and just then, something that happened thousands of kilometers to the east had shifted the balance in Syria in a way no one expected.

56
00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:27.800
In August 1259, in the Sichuan region of southwestern China, Möngke, the Great Khan of the Mongol Empire, died while besieging a Song fortress.

57
00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.130
The sources disagree on the cause of his death.

58
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.940
Some say it was dysentery, others cholera.

59
00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:39.870
One account holds that he was wounded by a projectile hurled from the fortress walls.

60
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:45.920
What is certain is that Möngke was Hulagu's elder brother, and that his death reopened the empire's greatest problem.

61
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.130
That problem was succession.

62
00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:56.900
In Mongol tradition, a new Great Khan was chosen at a kurultai, an assembly attended by members of the dynasty and the senior commanders.

63
00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.710
Two of Möngke's brothers put themselves forward for the throne.

64
00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:04.680
One was Kublai, on the Chinese front.

65
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.010
The other was Ariq Böke, in Mongolia.

66
00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:13.580
By the time the news reached Hulagu in Syria, the year 1260 had already begun.

67
00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:20.110
The Mongol postal system was fast, but news traveling from one end of the world to the other still took months.

68
00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.880
Hulagu took the bulk of his army and withdrew east, to Azerbaijan.

69
00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.450
For a long time, the succession struggle was thought to be the only reason for this withdrawal.

70
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:37.140
Today, historians point to another reason as well, and it may have been the more important one: pasture.

71
00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:43.350
A Mongol cavalryman did not set out on campaign with a single horse, but with several spare mounts.

72
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:47.800
An army of tens of thousands of soldiers meant hundreds of thousands of animals.

73
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.610
The highlands of Azerbaijan and Iran could feed those herds.

74
00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:55.420
The parched summers of Syria could not.

75
00:06:55.420 --> 00:07:01.790
Hulagu's army had reached the limit of how far an empire of the steppe could be carried into a land that was not steppe.

76
00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:08.320
Hulagu left a force behind in Syria and placed at its head one of his trusted commanders, Kitbuqa.

77
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.330
The size of this force is disputed.

78
00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.260
Sources and historians give figures ranging from 10 thousand to 20 thousand.

79
00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.910
This was not an army of conquest but a garrison.

80
00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:24.520
Its task was to hold Syria and keep order until Hulagu returned.

81
00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.970
Kitbuqa came from the Naiman tribe and was a Nestorian Christian.

82
00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:33.260
Nestorianism was widespread among the Mongol aristocracy.

83
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:38.990
Hulagu's most influential wife, Doquz Khatun, was a Nestorian as well.

84
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.760
This detail had stirred excitement among the Christian communities of Syria.

85
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.570
Some saw the Mongols as deliverance from Muslim rule.

86
00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:54.260
Bohemond, Prince of Antioch, and Hetum, King of Cilician Armenia, had pledged their allegiance to the Mongols.

87
00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:01.750
According to some Christian sources, Bohemond and Hetum rode alongside the Mongol commanders as they entered Damascus.

88
00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.920
The scene was not well received in the West.

89
00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.370
The Pope excommunicated Bohemond for collaborating with the Mongols.

90
00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:14.140
For those weighing the situation in Cairo, the picture had now changed.

91
00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:18.110
The great army that had taken Baghdad and burned Aleppo was gone.

92
00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:23.840
What stood before them was a force that was still dangerous, but one it was conceivable to defeat.

93
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.330
They also knew this opportunity would not last forever.

94
00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:32.900
Hulagu could return, and the Mongol succession struggle might be settled within a few months.

95
00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.750
Qutuz faced yet another problem.

96
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.080
His army was divided.

97
00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:49.610
A few years earlier, in 1254, the sultan at the time, Aybak, had ordered the killing of Aqtay, leader of the elite Mamluk regiment known as the Bahriyya.

98
00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.260
Qutuz had been among those involved in that murder.

99
00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:59.150
The leading figures of the Bahriyya had fled Egypt and spent years wandering Syria much like mercenaries.

100
00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.160
The best known among them was Baybars.

101
00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.010
Baybars, too, was a Kipchak.

102
00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:13.620
As a boy, after the Mongols swept across the Kipchak steppe, he had been taken captive and sold at the slave market in Sivas, in Anatolia.

103
00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:20.310
According to contemporary accounts, he had a blemish in one eye, and for that reason he sold for a low price.

104
00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:24.440
He was tall, dark-skinned, and had a booming voice.

105
00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:33.530
He eventually entered the service of as-Salih Ayyub, and in 1250 he was among the Mamluk cavalry who halted the Crusader army of King Louis of France at Mansurah.

106
00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:37.020
The Mongol threat weighed on Baybars as well.

107
00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.270
He had watched the Ayyubid princes of Syria collapse one by one before the Mongols.

108
00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:48.800
In 1260, he sent word to Qutuz, asked for guarantees, and returned to Cairo.

109
00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.610
Qutuz accepted him and granted him land and rank.

110
00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:57.380
The two men did not trust each other, and in all likelihood both of them knew it.

111
00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:02.150
But a common enemy was enough to set an old blood feud aside, for a time.

112
00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:06.120
When Qutuz gathered his emirs, he heard objections.

113
00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.530
Some wanted to stay in Egypt and fight a defensive war, meeting the Mongols in the desert.

114
00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:18.700
According to al-Maqrizi, Qutuz responded that he would compel no one to follow him, but that he himself was setting out.

115
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:24.110
He added that those who stayed behind would answer for whatever befell the Muslim women and children.

116
00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:26.320
The emirs followed him.

117
00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.730
At the end of July 1260, the Mamluk army set out north from Cairo.

118
00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:42.900
Ahead of it lay the Sinai desert, beyond that Gaza, and beyond Gaza a third power that no one had reckoned with: the Crusaders, clinging to their last few cities along the Mediterranean coast.

119
00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:48.150
The vanguard under Baybars easily scattered the small Mongol force left at Gaza.

120
00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:52.280
Meanwhile, the main army had a critical decision to make.

121
00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:57.050
Kitbuqa's forces were deep inside Syria, near Baalbek.

122
00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.220
The Mamluks had two routes to reach him.

123
00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:04.030
One ran through the interior, along the Jordan Valley.

124
00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.320
The other followed the coast, passing through Crusader territory.

125
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:15.170
The coastal route was faster and better for supply, but it meant a Muslim army marching through the heart of a Christian kingdom.

126
00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:18.660
The Kingdom of Jerusalem was a kingdom in name only.

127
00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.430
Jerusalem had not been in Christian hands since 1244.

128
00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:32.680
The kingdom's center was Acre, on the coast, and real power lay not with a king but was divided among barons, merchant republics and the military orders.

129
00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:35.530
Qutuz sent envoys to Acre.

130
00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:39.500
He wanted an alliance, or at the very least, free passage.

131
00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:44.270
We know about the debate in Acre from the letters the Crusaders sent back to the West.

132
00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:48.560
On one side were those who sympathized with the Mongols.

133
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.210
But that sympathy was melting away fast that summer.

134
00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:58.100
In August, Julian, the lord of Sidon, had led a raid into Mongol territory.

135
00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.750
In the fighting, a nephew of Kitbuqa was killed.

136
00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:07.960
Kitbuqa's response was harsh: Mongol troops descended on Sidon and sacked the city.

137
00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:18.330
The barons of Acre had learned the bitter lesson that the Mongols were not a Christian-friendly ally, but above all an empire that treated everyone who refused to submit in exactly the same way.

138
00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:25.660
It was also known that the Mongols demanded submission from the Crusaders as well, and that the letters they sent were full of threats.

139
00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.310
On the other hand, they did not trust the Mamluks either.

140
00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:34.720
A Muslim army camped in arms before the city meant a sword held to Acre's own gates.

141
00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.890
In the end, a middle path was chosen.

142
00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:44.900
The Crusaders did not enter into a military alliance with the Mamluks, and they did not fight alongside them.

143
00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:49.830
But they allowed the army to pass through their territory and agreed to sell it provisions.

144
00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:55.240
Under those circumstances, neutrality was the greatest help the Mamluks could have been given.

145
00:12:55.240 --> 00:13:00.330
The Mamluk army camped outside the walls of Acre for several days.

146
00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:06.380
According to some sources, a number of the Mamluk emirs entered the city and were received as guests.

147
00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:11.470
This became a detail the Crusaders would recall with some regret in the years that followed.

148
00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:21.360
The Crusaders who watched the size and discipline of that army from the gates of Acre did not know that within a few decades, the same army would wipe them out of the Eastern Mediterranean entirely.

149
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.650
Qutuz also used this halt to restore his army's morale.

150
00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:31.700
He gathered his emirs and reminded them of what the Mongols had done, and of what losing this war would mean.

151
00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:34.390
There was no road back to Egypt.

152
00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:38.680
Behind them lay the desert; ahead of them, the Mongols.

153
00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:44.730
Kitbuqa, for his part, turned south when he learned that the Mamluks were advancing north.

154
00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:48.700
He crossed the Jordan River and entered the region of Galilee.

155
00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:52.350
For a Mongol commander, this was a logical move.

156
00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:57.120
For years, the Mongols had almost never lost against Muslim armies.

157
00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.290
Kitbuqa had no reason to wait for his enemy.

158
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:06.660
The two armies drew near each other south of Galilee, at the eastern end of the Jezreel Valley.

159
00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:12.070
Here, at the foot of Mount Gilboa, a spring flowed out of a rocky hillside.

160
00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:16.200
The Arabs called it Ain Jalut, the Spring of Goliath.

161
00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:21.770
According to local tradition, this was where David had defeated the giant Goliath with his sling.

162
00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:24.940
The tradition was probably not historical.

163
00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:29.230
The biblical story took place in a different valley.

164
00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:36.720
But for those who stood there that September morning, the meaning of the name was clear: the place where the small brings down the great.

165
00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:45.970
On the 3rd of September 1260, the 25th day of Ramadan in the year 658 of the Islamic calendar, the battle began early in the morning.

166
00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.660
The Mamluk soldiers were fasting.

167
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:58.390
What the sources say about numbers is uncertain, but most historians believe the two armies were roughly equal in size, and that the Mamluks may even have been slightly more numerous.

168
00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:01.880
This was not a situation the Mongols were used to.

169
00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:08.890
Their great victories had usually been won not through superior numbers, but through mobility, discipline and deception.

170
00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:15.740
At Ain Jalut, however, the army facing them was made up of soldiers from the same steppe tradition as their own.

171
00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:19.070
The Mamluks, too, were mounted archers.

172
00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.720
They, too, had spent their childhoods in the saddle.

173
00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:26.050
And they, too, knew what deception was.

174
00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.860
Qutuz hid his army among the hillsides and the folds of the valley.

175
00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:34.470
The first thing the Mongols saw was the vanguard under the command of Baybars.

176
00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:39.560
This force advanced, clashed with the Mongols, and then began to fall back.

177
00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:44.170
It was the oldest trick in steppe warfare: the feigned retreat.

178
00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:47.820
The Mongols themselves had used it for centuries.

179
00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:53.550
They would lure the enemy into pursuit, scatter him, and then lead him into an ambush.

180
00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:56.880
This time, it was the Mongols who took the bait.

181
00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:03.570
Kitbuqa's cavalry set off after the retreating vanguard and were drawn toward the main body of the Mamluk army.

182
00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:07.380
But the battle did not unfold along the straight line of a plan.

183
00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:11.830
The Mongol charge struck the Mamluk left wing and broke clean through it.

184
00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:14.520
The wing began to scatter.

185
00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:20.090
For a moment, it looked as though Ain Jalut would end like every other Mongol battle.

186
00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:24.060
Mamluk historians would tell of that moment for centuries.

187
00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:29.470
Qutuz tore off his helmet and flung it to the ground, so that his soldiers could see his face.

188
00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:37.280
Then he cried out three times, at the top of his voice, "Oh, my Islam!" and plunged into the heart of the fighting with his own guards.

189
00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:40.290
Part of this account may have been embellished later.

190
00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:47.940
But that the sultan put himself in danger and rallied the broken wing appears consistently in the accounts of contemporary witnesses.

191
00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.750
There was one more factor that would decide the outcome of the battle.

192
00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:58.440
Among the Mongol ranks stood al-Ashraf Musa, the Ayyubid ruler of Homs, together with his soldiers.

193
00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:02.890
Al-Ashraf had submitted to the Mongols and joined the campaign on their behalf.

194
00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:07.820
But before the battle, he had come to a secret understanding with Qutuz.

195
00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:12.590
At the critical moment of the fighting, he withdrew his forces and left the field.

196
00:17:12.590 --> 00:17:15.760
A gap opened in the Mongol line.

197
00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:22.770
Some Mamluk sources claim that in this battle the Mamluks used a kind of gunpowder hand weapon to frighten the horses.

198
00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:28.020
What these weapons were, and how effective they proved, remains a matter of debate.

199
00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:35.190
Perhaps they were nothing more than a few fire tubes; perhaps they were an exaggeration by historians of later centuries.

200
00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:38.520
What is certain is that they were not what won the battle.

201
00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:44.570
What won the battle was a cavalry army disciplined enough to withstand a sudden change of direction.

202
00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:50.620
Once the Mamluks regrouped and launched their counterattack, the Mongol army began to come apart.

203
00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:53.470
Kitbuqa refused to retreat.

204
00:17:53.470 --> 00:18:00.000
In Mongol tradition, for a commander to abandon his khan and flee was a greater shame than death itself.

205
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.530
He fought on alongside the units around him, until his horse was killed beneath him and he was taken prisoner.

206
00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:11.460
Kitbuqa's final moments are described by the Ilkhanid historian Rashid al-Din.

207
00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:17.670
This is a Mongol source, and it portrays Kitbuqa as a hero, so it must be read with care.

208
00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:22.120
Yet the scene it describes reflects how these two worlds saw one another.

209
00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:26.730
Brought before Qutuz, Kitbuqa declares that he does not fear death.

210
00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:36.460
He adds that he himself is a servant who remained loyal to his master until the end of his life, whereas the men standing before him had risen to the throne by murdering their own masters.

211
00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:38.510
Then he is beheaded.

212
00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:42.160
These words sound like a prophecy, but they were not one.

213
00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:46.450
They were a bitter observation on the very nature of the Mamluk state.

214
00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:51.380
And just how right that observation was would become clear in a very short time.

215
00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:56.470
The fleeing Mongol units withdrew eastward, toward Beisan.

216
00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:59.160
There they tried to regroup once more.

217
00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:05.850
But the second clash also ended in a Mamluk victory, and the Mongol forces pulled back beyond the Euphrates.

218
00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:10.140
Within a few days, Damascus was freed of its Mongol garrison.

219
00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.310
Aleppo followed soon after.

220
00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:16.320
Syria had changed hands in less than a week.

221
00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:21.730
It is often said that Ain Jalut was the Mongols' first defeat, but that is not true.

222
00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.580
The Mongols had been beaten before.

223
00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:33.510
Jalal al-Din Khwarazmshah, whom Qutuz claimed as his uncle, had routed a Mongol army at Parwan, in Afghanistan, in 1221.

224
00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:38.760
The Volga Bulgars, too, had ambushed a Mongol force in the 1220s.

225
00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:42.570
But those defeats had no lasting consequences.

226
00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:47.340
Each time, the Mongols had come back and taken their revenge.

227
00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.990
What set Ain Jalut apart was what came afterward.

228
00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:54.000
Qutuz entered Damascus as a liberator.

229
00:19:54.000 --> 00:20:02.770
He appointed governors to the cities of Syria, brought the Ayyubid princes who had submitted to the Mongols back under his authority, and distributed the spoils.

230
00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:08.660
Al-Ashraf Musa, who had gone over to Qutuz during the battle, was left in place at Homs.

231
00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:14.710
News of the victory was sent to Cairo, and the city began to decorate itself for the sultan's return.

232
00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:19.480
Meanwhile, far away in Azerbaijan, the news reached Hulagu.

233
00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:26.010
Hulagu's fury fell upon a prisoner in his hands: an-Nasir Yusuf, the fugitive ruler of Syria.

234
00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:31.740
After fleeing Damascus, an-Nasir had fallen into Mongol hands and been brought before Hulagu.

235
00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:36.350
For a time, Hulagu may have considered using him to govern Syria.

236
00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:40.000
The news from Ain Jalut put an end to that idea.

237
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:43.330
An-Nasir Yusuf was executed.

238
00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:52.420
And so the last great Syrian ruler of the Ayyubid dynasty founded by Saladin paid the price for the victory of the Mamluks, who had done what he himself could not.

239
00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:57.510
The tension between Qutuz and Baybars resurfaced immediately after the victory.

240
00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:05.160
According to most sources, Baybars asked for the governorship of Aleppo as a reward for his role in the battle.

241
00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:07.370
Qutuz refused.

242
00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:15.660
Perhaps he thought it would be dangerous to leave so powerful an emir as Baybars in northern Syria, far from Egypt, with soldiers of his own.

243
00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:21.550
Or perhaps there was a score between the two men that had never truly been settled since the killing of Aqtay.

244
00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:26.640
Whatever the reason behind Qutuz's refusal, its consequences were grave.

245
00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:30.770
In October, Qutuz set out with his army to return to Egypt.

246
00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:37.300
As they neared the edge of the Sinai desert, on Egypt's eastern frontier, the sultan organized a hunt.

247
00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:44.150
This was something Mamluk rulers often did: both a pastime and a way of spending time among their soldiers.

248
00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:50.040
The details of what happened vary somewhat from source to source, but the broad outline is the same.

249
00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:54.650
During the hunt, Baybars and a group of emirs with him approached the sultan.

250
00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:57.660
Baybars asked Qutuz for a favor.

251
00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:02.430
According to some accounts, he asked for a woman from among the Mongol captives.

252
00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.960
Qutuz agreed.

253
00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:09.570
Baybars bent down to kiss the sultan's hand, and gripped it tightly.

254
00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:13.060
This was the signal the other emirs had been waiting for.

255
00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:16.070
Swords and daggers were drawn.

256
00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:18.920
Qutuz was killed in the saddle.

257
00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:22.890
The date was the 24th of October 1260.

258
00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:27.180
A little more than seven weeks had passed since Ain Jalut.

259
00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:34.990
When the conspirators returned to the sultan's tent, it was said that by Mamluk custom the man who killed the sultan should take his place.

260
00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:37.040
Baybars took the throne.

261
00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:47.570
He gave himself the title al-Malik al-Zahir, "the victorious king." When he entered Cairo, the decorations hung to welcome Qutuz were still up in the city's streets.

262
00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.900
The people did not cheer the sultan who had won the battle.

263
00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:53.590
They cheered the man who had killed him.

264
00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:57.720
The death of Qutuz held the whole contradiction of the Mamluk state.

265
00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:04.890
These soldiers had been bought as slaves and raised with no family ties, loyal only to their masters and to one another.

266
00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:08.060
That made them superb soldiers.

267
00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:13.310
It also created a system in which power was not inherited but won by the sword.

268
00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:19.360
The words Kitbuqa spoke before he died came true at the end of Qutuz's own story.

269
00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:24.450
In the years after Ain Jalut, the Mongols came into Syria several more times.

270
00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:34.820
In 1281, at the Second Battle of Homs, and in 1303, at Marj al-Saffar near Damascus, they met the Mamluk armies, and each time they were driven back.

271
00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:44.070
Only once, in 1299, did they defeat the Mamluks and take Damascus for a short time, but they could not hold it.

272
00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.440
The next summer they withdrew.

273
00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:53.130
The Euphrates River became, roughly speaking, the border between two worlds for more than half a century.

274
00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:57.420
Why that border held is a question historians still argue about today.

275
00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:04.270
One school sees Ain Jalut as the moment the Mongols first faced an army that fought with their own weapons and their own tactics.

276
00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:09.840
The Mamluks had come from the steppe, and they knew steppe warfare as well as the Mongols did.

277
00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:13.970
Other historians say this is only half the story.

278
00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:18.260
The real issue was the one we saw earlier: pasture.

279
00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:22.070
Syria could not feed the horses of a large Mongol army.

280
00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:29.080
Whenever the Mongols came to the region, they had to bring either a small army or a large one for only a short time.

281
00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:33.050
The Mamluk army they faced at Ain Jalut was not only skilled.

282
00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:35.900
It was also fighting at home.

283
00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:39.230
Then there was the division among the Mongols themselves.

284
00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:46.080
The succession struggle that began with Möngke's death ended the last period in which the empire answered to a single khan.

285
00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:52.130
Kublai won in the end, but by then the empire had in practice split into separate, independent khanates.

286
00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:57.060
One was the Ilkhanate, centered on Iran and founded by Hulagu.

287
00:24:57.060 --> 00:25:01.830
Another was the Golden Horde, which ruled the Kipchak steppe.

288
00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:06.120
And Berke, khan of the Golden Horde, had become a Muslim.

289
00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:10.250
Berke never forgave Hulagu for destroying Baghdad and killing the caliph.

290
00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:14.700
The two khanates were also in dispute over pastures in the Caucasus.

291
00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:18.510
In 1262, open war broke out between them.

292
00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:22.000
For the first time, Mongols were fighting Mongols.

293
00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:26.290
Baybars saw the opportunity at once and made an alliance with Berke.

294
00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:30.900
Envoys traveled back and forth between Cairo and the Golden Horde.

295
00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:34.070
The alliance also had a practical side.

296
00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:40.920
Most of the slave soldiers who filled the Mamluk army came from the lands of the Golden Horde, from the Kipchak steppe.

297
00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:47.610
This trade in people, stretching from the steppe to Egypt, was now part of a formal diplomatic relationship.

298
00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:53.340
Baybars found one more way to give legitimacy to the throne he had won by killing Qutuz.

299
00:25:53.340 --> 00:26:00.350
In 1261, in Cairo, he proclaimed as caliph a member of the Abbasid dynasty who had fled Baghdad.

300
00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:03.040
This caliph had no political power.

301
00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:08.930
But he gave the Mamluk sultans a powerful symbol for their claim to be the protectors of the Islamic world.

302
00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:17.860
The caliphate, snuffed out when Baghdad fell, began to live again in Cairo as a shadow, and that shadow would last for about 250 years.

303
00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:22.950
Perhaps the longest-lasting result of Ain Jalut was how long the thing that began there endured.

304
00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:28.840
The Mamluk state had been founded in 1250 and had proven itself in 1260.

305
00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:34.570
It survived until 1517, when the Ottoman sultan Selim the First took Cairo.

306
00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:40.780
For those 267 years, Egypt remained a country the Mongol invasions never reached.

307
00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:46.350
While the libraries of Baghdad, Merv and Nishapur burned, Cairo's libraries grew.

308
00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:55.600
With its madrasas, its mosques and its manuscripts, Mamluk Cairo became one of the greatest centers of learning and culture in the Islamic world.

309
00:26:55.600 --> 00:27:04.530
Historians like al-Maqrizi, whose record of Hulagu's letter we began with, could describe the period in such detail because of this unbroken continuity.

310
00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:08.660
In the end, the Mongols changed too.

311
00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:14.390
In 1295, the Ilkhanid ruler Ghazan Khan became a Muslim.

312
00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:19.320
Hulagu's descendants had become rulers of the faith whose center he had destroyed.

313
00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:22.810
The war with the Mamluks went on for a while longer.

314
00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:29.020
But now it was a border war between two Muslim states, not a civilization's struggle to survive.

315
00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:33.470
In 1323, the two sides signed a peace.

316
00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:36.960
It was 63 years after Ain Jalut.

317
00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:41.410
The story of Ain Jalut gathered together all the irony of a century.

318
00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:48.260
The Mongols had destroyed the Kipchak steppe, and the children they drove out of it became the army in Cairo that would stop them.

319
00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:55.590
The name of a small spring with no place in history, carrying the distant echo of a legend, became the name of a victory.

320
00:27:55.590 --> 00:28:00.040
And the sultan who won that victory did not live long enough to celebrate it.

321
00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:05.770
Qutuz's tomb was dug beside a road on the edge of Egypt, close to the place where he was killed.

322
00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:10.220
Later generations remembered him as the hero of Ain Jalut.

323
00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:13.390
Baybars ruled for 17 years.

324
00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:21.040
He took the Crusader castles one by one, fought the Mongols, and went down in history as perhaps the greatest of all the Mamluk sultans.

325
00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:24.850
The victory won at the Spring of Goliath belonged to both men.

326
00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:29.620
But once again, the point of a sword decided who would tell the story.

327
00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:33.270
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

328
00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:36.920
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
